PY  - 2026
JO  - Bioresource Technology
VL  - 453
DO  - 10.1016/j.biortech.2026.134703
AB  - Tetracycline is widely present in sludge, and its residual concentration can vary substantially under different contamination scenarios. Nanobubble water (NBW) has been shown to promote sludge anaerobic digestion (AD), but its effects on tetracycline removal and changes in antibiotic resistance genes (ARGs) during sludge AD remain unclear. This study evaluated the effects of NBW on sludge AD under different tetracycline concentrations (0, 2, and 20 mg L-1). The addition of NBW reduced tetracycline concentrations by 10.4%-23.1% and ARG abundance by 1.8%-14.3%, while mitigating the potential risks associated with ARG transmission. In addition, NBW may enhance antioxidant enzyme activities by upregulating the abundance of antioxidant-related functional genes, thereby strengthening the antioxidant capacity of the system. At the same time, NBW reduced the abundance of type IV secretion system-related functional genes, suggesting a reduced dissemination potential of ARGs associated with mobile genetic elements (MGEs). These changes may contribute to the reduction of ARGs during sludge AD. Overall, NBW improved the removal efficiency of tetracycline and ARGs during sludge AD, providing novel perspectives for mitigating the potential environmental risks associated with the final digestate.
